+**Parameters:**
+*   **Vicinity Radius:** The number of neighbor layers checked for the vicinity flag. Typically 1 (6-neighbor) or 3 (26-neighbor) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Skip Step Size:** The step size used in low-precision mode. Often equal to the voxel size or a multiple thereof [S1, unofficial].
 
-### Parameters
-- **Step Size ($\Delta t_{fine}$)**: Typically the voxel diagonal length or a fraction thereof to ensure no data is missed in vicinity regions [G].
-- **Connectivity**: 6-connected or 26-connected neighborhood for vicinity flag assignment [G]. 26-connected is more robust but increases preprocessing cost [G].
+**Edge Cases:**
+*   **Precision Loss:** The low-precision algorithm must not overshoot the vicinity region. If the step size is too large, the ray might skip over a vicinity voxel, leading to visual artifacts [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Handling:** The transition between low- and high-precision modes must be smooth to avoid sampling gaps or overlaps [S1, unofficial].
 
-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-- **Precision Loss**: The "low-precision" skip must be calculated accurately to avoid skipping over vicinity voxels [G]. Floating-point errors in ray parameter updates can cause jitter or missed samples at boundaries [G].
-- **Degenerate Input**: Volumes with no data voxels result in immediate termination [G]. Volumes with no empty voxels result in full high-precision traversal (no speedup) [G].
-- **Boundary Artifacts**: If the vicinity flag is not set correctly, rays may jump over thin structures, causing holes or artifacts [G].
